  {
   "type": "spec_point",
   "id": "spcpt_P4nX46W4vRP7bBfR",
   "name": "Collisions - Multiple Collisions",
   "definition": "",
   "video_id": "881898869"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 2,
   "text": "Collisions - Multiple Collisions"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Can there be multiple collisions?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- After **two objects collide** it is possible that one (or both) of them **collides with something else** such as\n\n  - A **third object**\n  - A **wall** (perpendicular to the motion)\n- Deal with **each collision separately** and use the steps for direct collisions"
  },
  {
   "type": "heading",
   "level": 4,
   "text": "Can there be a second collision between the original two objects?"
  },
  {
   "type": "bullets",
   "md": "- Let A, B and C be three objects travelling in the **same straight line** and suppose **A and B collide directly** and subsequently **B and C collide directly**\n- After the collisions between **A and B** and **B and C** there will be a **second collision** between **A and B if**:\n\n  - **One** is **stationary** and the **other** is **travelling towards** it\n  - Both are **travelling in opposite** directions **towards** each other\n  - Both are **travelling in the same** direction and the **one in front is slower** than the one behind\n- The process is **similar** if object **C is a wall**\n\n  - After **B collides with the wall** its **direction will be reversed** so it will be travelling **towards A**\n  - B will **collide with A** again if its **velocity in that direction **is **greater** than the velocity of A in that direction\n  - To help you work out the speed of B after hitting the wall you will be given extra information such as the **impulse** exerted by the wall or the **coefficient of restitution**"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "worked-example",
   "label": "Worked Example",
   "md": "Three uniform balls $A,B$ and $C$ of equal radius, and mass 0.1 kg, 0.2 kg and 0.3 kg respectively, can move along the same straight line on a smooth horizontal table with $B$ in the middle of $A$ and $C$. $A$ and $B$ are projected towards each other in opposite directions with speed $10ms^{-1}$ and $2ms^{-1}$respectively while $C$ is at rest. $A$ and $B$ collide directly which does not change the direction of motion of $A$ and subsequently $A$ moves with speed $1ms^{-1}$.\n\n(a) Show that the speed of $B$ immediately after it collides with $A$ is $2.5ms^{-1}$ .\n\n(b) In the subsequent motion, $B$ collides directly with $C$. Immediately after this collision, $C$ moves with speed $1.5ms^{-1}$. Determine if there will be a second collision between $A$ and $B$.\n\n**Answer:**\n\n![4-1-2-collisions---multiple-example-solution](assets/d0387efdc965-4-1-2-collisions-multiple-example-solution.png)"
  },
  {
   "type": "callout",
   "variant": "exam-tip",
   "label": "Exam Hint",
   "md": "- These questions can be difficult to visualise in your head so draw simple diagrams to show each collision.\n- Use common sense, think how many possible (or impossible) ways there are for objects to move after the first collision. You will often have to consider the speed of one or more objects to decide if a second or third collision is possible.\n- These questions can involve lots of algebra, negatives and inequalities so do not rush them as you might make a silly mistake which can affect subsequent parts."
  }
 ]
}